// Exercises: `sandbox`, `stop`. `sandbox worker(args)` runs `worker` as a
// real, separate OS process (not a thread -- a fresh `nirdosha` invocation
// that re-interprets this same source file) and returns an affine
// `sandbox` handle. `stop` terminates it (killing it if still running)
// and yields its OS exit code.
//
// This first layer (SANDBOXING.md) has no typed IPC yet -- a sandboxed
// function's return value never reaches the caller, only its exit status
// does, so `worker` must declare `-> unit` and take only plain scalar
// parameters (an integer type or `bool`); typeck.rs rejects anything else
// (see tests/sandbox.rs for the rejected programs).
//
// The interesting property this example demonstrates isn't the explicit
// `stop` below -- it's what happens if a program *doesn't* call it: a
// `sandbox` handle that goes out of scope without ever being stopped
// still kills its process (see `SandboxChild`'s `Drop` impl in
// interpreter.rs) -- no zombie process left behind just because a
// program forgot to clean up, the same "deterministic, not discipline-
// dependent" guarantee `box` already gives memory.
fn background_work() -> unit {
    while true {
    }
}

fn main() {
    let s: sandbox = sandbox background_work()
    let code: i64 = stop s
    print(code) // -1: background_work runs forever, so `stop` always
                // catches it still running and kills it
}
